<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">31 August in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">9 events that happened on 31 August, from 1056 to 1997.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1056">1056 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">970 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Empress Theodora dies, ending the Macedonian dynasty</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Theodora Porphyrogenita, Byzantine empress since 1042 and sole ruler since 1055, died childless after a sudden gastric illness. She was the last sovereign of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a>, which had ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> since 867.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty">Macedonian dynasty</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1218">1218 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">808 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Al-Kamil becomes Ayyubid sultan of Egypt</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">Al-Kamil</a>, a nephew of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a>, became the fourth Ayyubid sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. During his reign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubids</a> defeated the Fifth Crusade, and in 1229 he surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> to the emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Frederick II</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-jaffa-1229">Treaty of Jaffa</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-kamil">al-Kamil</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1302">1302 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">724 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Peace of Caltabellotta ends the War of the Sicilian Vespers</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Frederick III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and Charles, Count of Valois, signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-caltabellotta">Peace of Caltabellotta</a>, ending the war between the Angevins and the Aragonese. Frederick kept the island for life as King of Trinacria, while the Angevin Charles II kept mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-caltabellotta">Peace of Caltabellotta</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1422">1422 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">604 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Henry V of England dies at Vincennes</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Henry V, victor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-agincourt">Agincourt</a> and heir to the French throne, died of dysentery aged 35. His nine-month-old son Henry VI inherited a dual monarchy that soon unravelled.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1886">1886 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">140 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">An earthquake devastates Charleston, South Carolina</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">A powerful earthquake struck Charleston, damaging most of its buildings and killing about 60 people. It remains the strongest recorded earthquake in the south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1888">1888 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">138 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Mary Ann Nichols is murdered in Whitechapel</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The body of Mary Ann Nichols was found in Buck's Row, the first of five victims generally attributed to 'Jack the Ripper'. The killer was never identified.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1939">1939 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">87 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">SS men stage a fake Polish attack at Gleiwitz</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">SS operatives seized the German radio station at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gliwice">Gleiwitz</a> and broadcast an anti-German message, posing as Polish raiders. Hitler used such incidents to justify invading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> the next morning.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1962">1962 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">64 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Trinidad and Tobago becomes independent</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinidad">Trinidad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobago">Tobago</a> gained independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-williams">Eric Williams</a> as prime minister. Oil and gas wealth made it one of the most prosperous nations in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1997">1997 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">29 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Diana, Princess of Wales dies in a Paris car crash</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Diana was fatally injured when her car crashed in the Pont de l'Alma tunnel while pursued by photographers. Her death provoked an extraordinary outpouring of public grief in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and beyond.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a></div></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 31 August <span class="n">7</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/emperor-zhangzong-of-jin"><span class="ph ph-person" aria-hidden="true"></span><span><span class="nm">Emperor Zhangzong of Jin</span><span class="tp">1168–1208</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jahangir"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jahangir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Jahangir</span><span class="tp">1569–1627</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinando-carlo-gonzaga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ferdinando-carlo-gonzaga.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ferdinando Carlo Gonzaga</span><span class="tp">1652–1708</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ramon-castilla"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ramon-castilla.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ramón Castilla</span><span class="tp">1797–1867</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/maria-montessori"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/maria-montessori.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Maria Montessori</span><span class="tp">1870–1952</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmina-of-the-netherlands"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/wilhelmina-of-the-netherlands.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Wilhelmina of the Netherlands</span><span class="tp">1880–1962</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ramon-magsaysay"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ramon-magsaysay.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ramon Magsaysay</span><span class="tp">1907–1957</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 31 August <span class="n">5</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/otomo-no-tabito"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/otomo-no-tabito.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ōtomo no Tabito</span><span class="tp">665–731</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-castile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/sancho-iii-of-castile.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Sancho III of Castile</span><span class="tp">c. 1134–1158</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/arthur-phillip"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/arthur-phillip.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Arthur Phillip</span><span class="tp">1738–1814</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/norman-kirk"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/norman-kirk.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Norman Kirk</span><span class="tp">1923–1974</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/urho-kekkonen"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/urho-kekkonen.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Urho Kekkonen</span><span class="tp">1900–1986</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
